<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Man of 82 with newly diagnosed prostate adenocarcinoma, Grade Group 3, PSA 6.4 ng/mL, cT1c, NCCN unfavorable intermediate risk, found after a rising PSA on monitoring. Please advise on curative treatment, including radiotherapy with short-term androgen deprivation, and whether observation is reasonable at his age. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within four to six weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Do you agree with the risk group, and is staging complete with the PSMA PET/CT?</item><item>What are his curative options at 82, and is observation a reasonable choice?</item><item>If radiotherapy is chosen, should androgen deprivation be added, for how long, and how should it start?</item><item>Does his earlier prostate operation change the choice of technique or his risk of urinary side effects?</item><item>What monitoring is needed during and after treatment, including PSA follow-up, and which parts should primary care manage?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Mandela is seen at the request of Dr. Mokoena for newly diagnosed prostate cancer. A rise in PSA on routine monitoring led to an MRI and a targeted biopsy, which showed Grade Group 3 adenocarcinoma in 3 of 14 cores. He has mild urinary frequency, with an IPSS of 7, and no bone pain, weight loss, or blood in the urine. Decades ago part of his prostate was removed for benign enlargement. He had tuberculosis in the past, treated, with no symptoms since. He is independent, walks every morning, and keeps a full schedule of work and travel. He wants to understand all the options before deciding. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Nelson Rolihlahla Mandela was born in Mvezo, in the Eastern Cape, on 18 July 1918. [D]</item><item>Mandela was convicted with seven co-accused in the Rivonia Trial on 11 June 1964 and sentenced to life imprisonment the next day. He was held on Robben Island, moved to Pollsmoor Prison on 31 March 1982 and to Victor Verster Prison near Paarl on 7 December 1988, and was released from Victor Verster on 11 February 1990. [D]</item><item>In 1985 Mandela had prostate surgery while imprisoned. His urologist said in July 2001 that the gland had been enlarged and was causing a urinary blockage, and that part of it was removed. [D]</item><item>Mandela was taken to hospital on 12 August 1988 and diagnosed with tuberculosis; public reports describe it as early tuberculosis, treated at the Constantiaberg Medi-clinic, after which he was moved to Victor Verster Prison. [D]</item><item>His doctors said publicly in 2001 that he had been monitored regularly with the prostate-specific antigen (PSA) test, that a slight rise in the PSA led to a biopsy of the prostate, and that the biopsy confirmed microscopic cancer within the gland. News reports said the raised blood level was first found in November 2000. [D]</item><item>Mandela was inaugurated as South Africa&apos;s first democratically elected president on 10 May 1994 and stepped down in 1999 after one term. [D]</item></list><paragraph>4 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

